                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Hades 2 does many things better, and many things worse.\nMy views after having completed the original with 100% achievements.\n\nThis game is a lot richer than its predecessor and has many more fun mechanics and items to play around with. I also appreciate that there are two different paths, as it breaks the monotony and can be refreshing. The same goes for the open-field area in the underground.\n\nHowever, the top side is unfortunately very bad, in my opinion, for multiple reasons. Specifically, the Mount and Summit. The first two regions are great, especially the first one. Once you start the mountain climb, though, it falls apart. Yes, the difficulty is ridiculously dialed up, but that is not my main issue.\n\nMy main issue is the boss and level design.\n\nSince it is essentially two regions in one, it becomes very monotonous, and that is made considerably worse by the fact that almost every encounter has the same enemies and takes place in the same kind of boxed-in room. It is tight and creates an absolute mess of visual clutter and enemy numbers without giving you much freedom of movement, which seriously hampers any kind of strategy beyond spraying and praying for the most part.\n\nThe bosses are also very lame.\n\nPrometheus will literally stop the fight to play a memory game with you, which is both concentration and immersion-breaking, while also being far too punishing if you miss one, or god forbid, multiple sequences. It can burn through a bunch of your revives. That sequence should automatically end if you trigger a Death Defiance.\n\nThe Summit throws multiple hard-hitting bosses at you without giving you any real chance to recuperate or heal before going into the final boss. And that final boss is atrocious.\n\nThe fight itself is long and boring. It is essentially a damage sponge where you find yourself running up, throwing a cast and getting a few hits in, then running around, and repeating that ad infinitum. It is so lame that after beating him the first few times, I was reluctant and dreading having to redo that fight. It is simply not fun.\n\nThere is challenging difficulty, and then there is cheap difficulty. In a lot of cases, the Summit falls into the latter. That is especially disappointing because the vast majority of the rest of the game is fantastic.\n\nThere is so much I want to love about this sequel, but that Mount + Summit section really puts a sour taste in my mouth.\n\nUnrelated side note: I kind of wish all the small talk you have with the gods and companions would actually result in something definitive. It can be fun from a lore perspective, but at the end of the day, you know nothing will come out of all the talking. It is just talk for the sake of talking.\n\nIt is interesting at first, but gets annoying as you go on. This was somewhat of a pet peeve of mine since the first game, to be fair. I end up skipping most of it after a couple dozen hours. They speak of solutions and make promises, but you know it will lead to nothing because it cannot. There is no closure, and therefore it gradually loses its value.\n\nIf they ever make a third one, I really hope those conversations occasionally lead to something concrete and actionable.",
